WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.720
A coast redwood lifts water more than one hundred metres, with no heart or other

2
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.900
pump organ. Where does the force come from?

3
00:00:12.883 --> 00:00:19.063
Plant biologists track water with one number, its water potential Pure water at

4
00:00:19.063 --> 00:00:21.163
atmospheric pressure is set at zero.

5
00:00:22.703 --> 00:00:26.443
Dissolve a solute in open water, and the number drops below zero.

6
00:00:27.863 --> 00:00:31.323
Water crossing a membrane moves toward lower water potential.

7
00:00:32.467 --> 00:00:35.347
Now line up a plant, with illustrative values.

8
00:00:36.547 --> 00:00:40.687
The soil sits a little below zero, the root lower, the leaf lower still.

9
00:00:41.967 --> 00:00:43.627
The air sits far below them all.

10
00:00:44.567 --> 00:00:46.927
So water runs downhill all the way.

11
00:00:48.027 --> 00:00:51.207
The biggest drop by far is from the leaf to the dry air.

12
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:57.880
Inside a leaf, water evaporates from the wet walls of the mesophyll cells.

13
00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:00.980
These inner cells do photosynthesis.

14
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.440
The vapour diffuses out to the drier air.

15
00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:10.663
As water leaves, the water still held in the tiny pores of the walls is held more

16
00:01:10.663 --> 00:01:13.943
tightly. Its pressure falls below zero.

17
00:01:15.263 --> 00:01:16.583
That is tension, a pull.

18
00:01:17.633 --> 00:01:21.093
Negative pressure means the water is stretched, not squeezed.

19
00:01:22.673 --> 00:01:25.973
Picture a sealed syringe full of water, its plunger pulled back.

20
00:01:27.213 --> 00:01:31.193
If no bubble forms, the water pulls on the plunger instead of pushing.

21
00:01:32.367 --> 00:01:34.547
Why does the pull not just snap the water?

22
00:01:35.947 --> 00:01:38.927
Water molecules cling to each other through hydrogen bonds.

23
00:01:40.307 --> 00:01:44.527
This cohesion lets a thin column in a narrow tube be pulled like a rope.

24
00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:50.250
The tubes are xylem cells called tracheids and vessel elements.

25
00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.070
They are dead when they work, with their contents gone.

26
00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:57.490
They leave a hollow pathway from root to leaf.

27
00:01:58.983 --> 00:02:03.803
Henry Dixon and John Joly proposed this in the 1890s.

28
00:02:04.583 --> 00:02:09.463
Evaporation pulls, cohesion passes the pull down, and the column stays under

29
00:02:09.463 --> 00:02:10.463
tension.

30
00:02:11.017 --> 00:02:12.377
Height adds a cost.

31
00:02:13.677 --> 00:02:19.017
Each metre takes about 0.01 MPa more tension, just to hold a still column.

32
00:02:20.337 --> 00:02:25.997
At the top of a 110 m redwood, gravity alone adds over 1 MPa.

33
00:02:27.117 --> 00:02:29.177
Roots can push water too, a little.

34
00:02:30.517 --> 00:02:34.757
If xylem water were pushed from below, what would happen when you cut it on a sunny

35
00:02:34.757 --> 00:02:35.757
afternoon?

36
00:02:41.883 --> 00:02:43.563
Pushed water would spurt out.

37
00:02:44.743 --> 00:02:50.303
It does not. Cut the xylem of a transpiring tree and air is drawn in.

38
00:02:51.443 --> 00:02:52.843
The water was under tension.

39
00:02:53.983 --> 00:02:55.743
So the force comes from the top.

40
00:02:57.223 --> 00:03:01.903
Evaporation lowers the pressure in the leaf, and the water column below is pulled up

41
00:03:01.903 --> 00:03:02.903
toward it.

42
00:03:03.367 --> 00:03:08.187
Water leaves for lower water potential, and the dry air is lowest of all.

43
00:03:09.407 --> 00:03:11.167
So the xylem is pulled, not pushed.
